1
00:00:05,249 --> 00:00:11,650
Near the end of Moby-Dick, on a calm blue morning, Captain Ahab almost turns his ship for home.

2
00:00:11,985 --> 00:00:15,412
This guide covers the novel and a version where he does.

3
00:00:16,441 --> 00:00:20,548
Hello, and welcome to Unwritten Chapters, Study Edition.

4
00:00:20,858 --> 00:00:25,159
In the next few minutes, I'll walk you through Moby-Dick, by Herman Melville.

5
00:00:25,484 --> 00:00:30,318
It's a quick guide you can use for class, a book club, or your own curiosity.

6
00:00:30,659 --> 00:00:34,998
The full guide, with ten museum and archive sources, is linked below.

7
00:00:36,890 --> 00:00:43,770
A young sailor who asks us to call him Ishmael signs on to a whaling ship from Nantucket called the Pequod.

8
00:00:44,176 --> 00:00:51,987
Its captain, Ahab, has lost a leg to a giant white sperm whale called Moby Dick, and he's sworn to find it.

9
00:00:52,490 --> 00:00:56,492
He nails a gold coin to the mast for whoever sees the whale first.

10
00:00:57,320 --> 00:01:05,031
The book follows the long voyage across the oceans, mixing adventure with chapters about whales, ships and the sea.

11
00:01:05,428 --> 00:01:13,267
Near the end, in a chapter called "The Symphony," Ahab talks gently with his first mate, Starbuck, about the wife and child he

12
00:01:13,267 --> 00:01:14,325
left at home.

13
00:01:14,742 --> 00:01:17,025
Starbuck begs him to turn back.

14
00:01:17,435 --> 00:01:19,150
Ahab won't turn back.

15
00:01:19,565 --> 00:01:25,148
The Pequod finds the white whale, and after a three-day chase the ship is destroyed.

16
00:01:25,499 --> 00:01:28,225
Only Ishmael survives to tell the story.

17
00:01:29,861 --> 00:01:31,666
Now for the story.

18
00:01:31,983 --> 00:01:36,021
Our sleep story for this book is called "Ahab Turns for Home."

19
00:01:36,347 --> 00:01:39,099
Here's how it unfolds, chapter by chapter.

20
00:01:39,384 --> 00:01:40,445
Chapter one.

21
00:01:40,689 --> 00:01:41,698
Loomings.

22
00:01:42,352 --> 00:01:45,384
Ishmael goes to sea whenever he feels gloomy.

23
00:01:45,684 --> 00:01:50,633
One cold December he signs on to the Pequod, a whaling ship from Nantucket.

24
00:01:50,873 --> 00:01:54,372
Her captain, Ahab, has a leg made of whalebone.

25
00:01:54,641 --> 00:01:58,708
He lost the real one to a huge white whale called Moby Dick.

26
00:01:59,049 --> 00:02:03,455
Ahab nails a gold coin to the mast for whoever sights the whale first.

27
00:02:03,773 --> 00:02:05,128
The crew cheers.

28
00:02:05,467 --> 00:02:11,287
Only Starbuck, the careful first mate, stays quiet and thinks of his wife and son.

29
00:02:11,517 --> 00:02:16,579
Starbuck came to sea to hunt whales for their oil, as an honest living.

30
00:02:16,961 --> 00:02:20,593
He didn't come to chase one whale across the world for revenge.

31
00:02:20,957 --> 00:02:26,042
He looks at the coin for a long time, then goes below to the picture of his family.

32
00:02:26,482 --> 00:02:27,548
Chapter two.

33
00:02:27,753 --> 00:02:28,876
The Mast-Head.

34
00:02:29,585 --> 00:02:34,090
The Pequod sails for months, around Africa and into the Pacific.

35
00:02:34,581 --> 00:02:38,728
Ishmael takes his turn at the masthead and drifts into daydreams.

36
00:02:39,401 --> 00:02:43,271
On calm golden days the sea is like glass.

37
00:02:44,114 --> 00:02:51,257
Starbuck thinks about the hill above Nantucket harbour, where his wife has promised to carry their little boy every morning to watch

38
00:02:51,257 --> 00:02:52,697
for his father's sail.

39
00:02:53,090 --> 00:02:56,020
Ahab paces the deck and looks at the coin.

40
00:02:56,457 --> 00:02:58,825
Ishmael admits he's a poor lookout.

41
00:02:59,354 --> 00:03:06,842
Up at the top of the mast, a hundred feet above the deck, he feels the whole ocean is a dream, and he's part of it.

42
00:03:07,168 --> 00:03:12,960
Melville writes about those calm days in chapters called "The Mast-Head" and "The Gilder."

43
00:03:13,728 --> 00:03:14,830
Chapter three.

44
00:03:15,135 --> 00:03:16,097
The Symphony.

45
00:03:16,490 --> 00:03:22,485
On a clear steel-blue morning, Ahab leans over the rail and drops a tear into the sea.

46
00:03:22,958 --> 00:03:28,482
He tells Starbuck about forty years at sea, and the wife and little boy he left behind.

47
00:03:28,912 --> 00:03:31,349
Starbuck begs him to turn for home.

48
00:03:31,852 --> 00:03:40,517
He tells Ahab about the hill, and the boy watching for a sail, and Ahab says he's seen mild blue days like this in Nantucket too.

49
00:03:40,971 --> 00:03:43,285
In the book, Ahab looks away.

50
00:03:43,585 --> 00:03:45,164
Tonight he doesn't.

51
00:03:45,528 --> 00:03:51,130
He calls the crew, the yards swing round, and the Pequod turns her bow toward home.

52
00:03:51,457 --> 00:03:56,608
Up at the masthead, Ishmael watches the long white wake curve away behind them.

53
00:03:57,053 --> 00:03:58,427
The Last Page.

54
00:03:58,860 --> 00:04:02,501
Ahab gives the gold coin to Starbuck, for the boy.

55
00:04:03,084 --> 00:04:08,272
The ship sails the long way home, and the white whale swims on, free.

56
00:04:08,844 --> 00:04:13,370
One summer morning, a little boy on the hill above Nantucket sees a sail.

57
00:04:13,943 --> 00:04:19,990
Years later, Ishmael writes the whole story down, and begins it with the same three words.

58
00:04:21,831 --> 00:04:27,409
Ishmael. The narrator, a thoughtful young sailor who goes to sea when he feels gloomy.

59
00:04:28,004 --> 00:04:29,256
Captain Ahab.

60
00:04:29,523 --> 00:04:34,641
The Pequod's captain, who has a whalebone leg and wants revenge on the white whale.

61
00:04:35,161 --> 00:04:36,525
Starbuck.

62
00:04:36,859 --> 00:04:41,490
The careful first mate from Nantucket, who has a wife and young son at home.

63
00:04:42,185 --> 00:04:43,024
Queequeg.

64
00:04:43,266 --> 00:04:47,675
A harpooneer from a South Sea island, and Ishmael's closest friend.

65
00:04:48,060 --> 00:04:49,443
Moby Dick.

66
00:04:49,718 --> 00:04:53,190
The great white whale, famous all over the ocean.

67
00:04:54,995 --> 00:04:56,044
Obsession.

68
00:04:56,374 --> 00:04:59,146
Ahab can't let go of his anger at the whale.

69
00:04:59,386 --> 00:05:01,933
It pushes everything else out of his life.

70
00:05:02,699 --> 00:05:06,606
Home. Starbuck keeps thinking of his wife and son.

71
00:05:06,902 --> 00:05:11,494
The calm chapter "The Symphony" shows Ahab remembering home too.

72
00:05:11,867 --> 00:05:15,888
Choice. In the book, Ahab says he can't stop.

73
00:05:16,222 --> 00:05:19,781
Our story asks what happens if he chooses differently.

74
00:05:20,118 --> 00:05:21,663
The size of the sea.

75
00:05:22,016 --> 00:05:29,711
Melville fills the book with facts about whales and oceans to show how huge and mysterious the natural world is.

76
00:05:31,498 --> 00:05:32,221
So what's the what-if?

77
00:05:32,221 --> 00:05:35,814
Here's the turning point we changed.

78
00:05:36,374 --> 00:05:42,567
In our story, everything happens as it does in the book until that calm blue morning in "The Symphony."

79
00:05:43,045 --> 00:05:48,536
Starbuck begs Ahab to turn for home, and this time Ahab doesn't look away.

80
00:05:48,965 --> 00:05:51,543
He calls the crew and orders the ship about.

81
00:05:52,346 --> 00:05:58,219
Everything after that moment is new, but the characters still act the way their authors wrote them.

82
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:02,980
Before we go, here's a little about who wrote it.

83
00:06:03,746 --> 00:06:07,241
Herman Melville was born in New York in 1819.

84
00:06:07,539 --> 00:06:14,039
As a young man he sailed on a whaling ship to the Pacific and later jumped ship in the Marquesas Islands.

85
00:06:14,254 --> 00:06:18,491
He wrote Moby-Dick at his farmhouse, Arrowhead, in Massachusetts.

86
00:06:18,840 --> 00:06:23,210
The book sold poorly in his lifetime, and he died almost forgotten.

87
00:06:25,134 --> 00:06:30,060
In the 1840s and 1850s, whaling was a huge industry.

88
00:06:30,319 --> 00:06:38,248
Whale oil lit lamps across America and Europe, and ships from Nantucket and New Bedford stayed at sea for three or four years at

89
00:06:38,248 --> 00:06:41,286
a time, sailing all the way around the world.

90
00:06:43,272 --> 00:06:44,671
Want to see the real thing?

